From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Fri, 14 Oct 1994 20:18:48 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-10-236] SCI: Many-Worlds FAQ

Michael Price:
> For instance, how do you test the statement that a particular
> coin is unbiased?  The only way I know is to toss it repeatedly.  Only
> as the number of trials goes to infinity we can gain complete 
> assurance  that the coin is fair (or not).

It is never possible to gain complete assurance, even at the limit
of infinity.  Moreover finite sequences can be truly random,
but it is impossible prove that a given sequence of nontrivial 
length is truly random, or that future extensions of that 
sequence will be truly random.  These facts are due to the 
fact that all statistical measures are incomplete.  They measure 
certain common attributes of randomness.  They can find 
regularity but they cannot rule it out.

One obvious example of an incomplete statistical measure is the
mean, which should be spin 1/2 for a random source of particles
with spin 0 or 1.  But if the spin source gives a sequence

010101010101010101010...

it would have the same mean as a perfectly random source.
For any other computable statistical test you can give to define
"randomness", I can give you a sequence that passes that
test but is, neverthless, quite regular and predictable.  Another 
example of predictable "random" sequences are the digits of 
pi, which pass traditional statistical tests for randomness, 
but are nevertheless quite regular.  If, for example, 
you XORd a long sequence of regular digits with some digits of
pi you computed, you wouldn't irrevocably hide the regularity
in the original digits: I would simply compute my own pi digits
and reverse the XOR (repeatedly until I hit your starting
point, if you didn't start at the first digit).

Sequences, such as a series of quantum attribute measurements,
are only truly random if there exists no algorithm with 
which we can compress the sequence.  This is an uncomputable 
problem: we can never prove that quantum phenomenon are truly
random.  We can only say that they are random to the best of
our current knowledge, and keep trying to increase that 
knowledge by tediously ruling out more classes of regularity
on larger batches of data.

According to Bell, Aspect, et. al, EPR experiments demonstrate
"superluminal effects", which (assuming the source is truly,
perfectly random) only show correlation between the sites,
not within sites.  Thus, subluminal transmission is needed to
show the correlations that indicate superluminal cause and
effect.  If regularity can be demonstrated in any "EPRable" 
phenomenon, by demonstrating an algorithm that gives rise
to a significant and predictable part of that quantum attribute
sequence, true FTL (and perhaps even chronology protection violation) 
seems to to become possible, without resort to exotic matter.  Now
some physicists might take this as proof that quantum
mechanics are random, but that proof is based on anti-FTL
and/or chronology protection axioms, not on statistical measures.
Also, some physicists might use incomplete statistical measures
as "proof" that information can't be transmitted, but such
a "proof" would be based on a misunderstanding of statistics,
information, and randomness.  The best we can say is that
nobody has figured out how to do it; we can't prove it is
impossible.

For more information on randomness and statistical tests, see
Li & Vitanyi, _An Introduction to Kolmogorov Complexity and
Its Applications_, esp. chapters 1 and 4.

