From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Fri, 14 Oct 1994 22:14:39 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-238] Searle on AI (Was: Challenge to Uploaders)

Well, I think Einstein is the wrong historical analogy for Searle.  I think
Ludwig von Mises is the right one.  Just as socialists went hysterical when
Mises proved the impossibility of rational economic calculation under pure
socialism, so Searle proved the impossibility of what he calls "strong AI."
Just as socialists quit talking about pure socialism and start talking about
"market socialism" when confronted with Mises' impossibility proof, so do
AI advocates stop talking about strong AI and start talking about something
else when confronted with Searle's Chinese Room argument - if they ever get
past attacking the argument on what ultimately come down to methodological
grounds.  I've come to the conclusion that AI advocates care more about
their methodology than they do about their subject matter.  They let their
methodology determine their subject matter, when it ought to be the other
way around.

Marvin Minsky's dismissal, for instance, amounts to: "How dare anyone get
in the way of our glorious research programs by asking that we consider an
inherently subjective phenomenon from a subjective point of view!?!" Hans
Moravec's statement is pretty much the same.

My answer is: no one's threatening your research programs.  Machines that
will be able to pass the Turing Tests will be great to have, and I welcome
the day that you develop them.  Computer simulations of the personalities
of particular people will be wonderful.

But just don't think you're going to get away with calling these tools
"intelligent" in the same way as people are with anyone lacking your
methodological prejudice who takes human intelligence seriously.  Don't think
that these critics of your work are evil, incompetent, or superfluous.  We
aren't.  We're just studying something else, and would like the terminology
used to reflect this distinction.

